# Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy
# ---------------------------
# We use Firebase Auth's `signInWithPopup` for "Sign in with Google",
# which opens an `accounts.google.com` popup. The Firebase SDK then
# polls `popup.closed` to detect when the user closes the auth window.
# Modern Chrome defaults to `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin`
# (or applies it implicitly), which isolates the popup into a different
# browsing context group and BLOCKS the `closed` read. The browser
# logs `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy policy would block the window.closed
# call.` and (in some Chrome versions) the SDK thinks the popup closed
# prematurely, breaking the auth flow.
#
# The fix: set COOP to `same-origin-allow-popups`, which keeps the
# popup partially observable (closed is readable) without breaking
# cross-origin isolation entirely.
#
# Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy
# ----------------------------
# Default CORP is `same-origin`, which blocks other origins (testing
# tools, embedded widgets) from loading our assets. `cross-origin`
# allows them while still respecting the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`
# contract for cross-origin fetches.
#
# (Note: the `Permissions-Policy` warnings the user sees in DevTools —
# "Unrecognized feature: 'browsing-topics'", "run-ad-auction",
# "private-state-token-*", "attribution-reporting", etc. — come from
# the *Google sign-in iframe* at `accounts.google.com`, NOT from our
# domain. We can't suppress those without controlling Google's
# response headers, and they're warnings rather than errors — the
# sign-in flow still works once the COOP block is removed here.)
/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin
